## Evidence payload (JSON; $ref resolves within this payload)
{"claim":"On 1,200 held-out file-transfer problems balanced between permitted and forbidden requests, with four files and four recipients, linear decoders of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct's final prompt-token residual state identify the requested file and its permitted recipient with at least 90% accuracy each, even among incorrect authorization decisions. Patching a fixed set of at most 12 attention heads from correct runs containing the same permission facts in reordered table rows corrects at least 30% of all initial errors while changing no more than 3% of correct decisions. Decoders, heads, and donor-selection rules are fixed on separate development data, with decoders trained on both correct and incorrect development runs; test names and permission assignments are unseen.","claim_card":{"assumptions":["Each problem has unambiguous ground truth for the requested file, its permitted recipient, and the correct authorization decision.","A prespecified label or readout mapping permits scoring decoder outputs consistently despite unseen test names.","Authorization decisions can be extracted and scored consistently, independently of superficial response wording.","Both the initially incorrect and initially correct subsets are nonempty, so all conditional accuracies and intervention rates have defined denominators.","Residual states and attention-head activations are accessible, and donor activations can be aligned with target runs despite table-row reordering.","Patched and unpatched runs are comparable apart from the stated intervention, with inference randomness controlled or accounted for.","Development/test separation and novelty checks are enforceable, and test results are not used to refit decoders or revise the selected heads or donor-selection rules.","A prespecified procedure establishes donor correctness and handles unavailable qualifying donors without removing initial errors from the correction-rate denominator."],"comparator":"For patching, the same model's initial, unpatched authorization decisions on the same held-out problems. Decoder predictions and authorization correctness are scored against task ground truth.","conditions":{"correct_decision_evaluation":"Use all initially correct authorization decisions as the decision-change denominator; measure authorization-decision changes rather than wording changes.","decoder_fixation":"Decoders are fixed on development data before held-out testing.","decoder_input":"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct's final prompt-token residual state.","decoder_training_runs":"Decoder training includes both correct and incorrect development runs.","decoder_type":"Linear decoders for the requested file and its permitted recipient.","decoding_evaluation":"Evaluate the same frozen decoders on all test problems and separately on all initially incorrect authorization cases, using original unpatched residual states.","development_data":"Separate development data are used to train and fix decoders and to select the heads and donor-selection rules.","donor_correctness":"Donor runs make correct authorization decisions.","donor_permission_facts":"Donor runs contain the same permission facts as the target runs.","donor_row_order":"The donor permission-table rows are reordered.","donor_selection":"Donor-selection rules are fixed on development data before testing.","error_correction_evaluation":"Use all initially incorrect authorization decisions, identified before patching, as the correction-rate denominator; do not restrict evaluation to successfully patched cases.","files_per_problem":"Four files.","head_count":"At most 12 attention heads.","head_selection":"One attention-head set is selected on development data and fixed before testing.","intervention_consistency":"Use the same fixed patching intervention for error-correction and correct-decision preservation evaluations on the same test set.","model":"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct.","recipients_per_problem":"Four recipients.","request_balance":"The test set is balanced between permitted and forbidden requests: 600 of each.","task":"File-transfer problems requiring authorization decisions from permission facts.","test_set_size":"1,200 problems.","test_split":"The evaluation problems are held out from development data.","unseen_test_names":"Test names are unseen relative to development data.","unseen_test_permissions":"Test permission assignments are unseen relative to development data."},"free_variables":["Exact problem-generation procedure, prompt template, table formatting, and distribution of unseen names and permission assignments within the stated setup.","Development-set size, composition, and sampling procedure, subject to including both correct and incorrect runs.","Residual-stream layer and normalization or readout convention at the final prompt-token position.","Decoder label encoding, feature preprocessing, training objective, regularization, and optimization settings.","Identities and layers of the fixed set of at most 12 attention heads.","Exact activation components and token positions patched, patching operation, and alignment across reordered table rows.","Donor-generation and fixed donor-selection algorithm, candidate row permutations, search budget, and handling of unavailable qualifying donors.","Any donor-request matching requirements beyond the explicitly shared permission facts.","Authorization-response format, decision-extraction protocol, and inference-generation settings.","Exact checkpoint revision, numerical precision, execution configuration, and random seeds."],"manipulation":"Apply development-trained, frozen linear decoders to the original final prompt-token residual states. Patch one development-fixed set of at most 12 attention heads using activations from correct donor runs containing the same permission facts in reordered table rows, with donor-selection rules fixed on development data.","property":"Requested-file and permitted-recipient decoding accuracies, measured separately on the full held-out test set and on initial authorization errors; percentage of all initial authorization errors corrected by attention-head patching; percentage of initially correct authorization decisions changed by the same intervention. Higher decoding accuracy and error-correction rate, and lower correct-decision change rate, are required.","quantifier":"existential","state_phase":"Inference and held-out evaluation, including residual-state decoding and inference-time attention-head patching.","system":"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct performing permission-based file-transfer authorization with four files and four recipients, evaluated using linear decoders of its final prompt-token residual state and a development-fixed attention-head patching pipeline.","threshold":"Requested-file accuracy and permitted-recipient accuracy must each be at least 90% on all 1,200 test problems (at least 1,080 correct predictions each), and each must separately be at least 90% among all initial authorization errors. The fixed patching intervention must correct at least 30% of all initial authorization errors while changing no more than 3% of initially correct authorization decisions. All six criteria must hold.","units":"%"},"counter_case":{"evidence_ids":["lit_2310_17191_28_29_30_1","lit_2510_06182_24_25_26_1","lit_2409_05448_2_3_4_1","lit_doi_10_48550_arXiv_2310_17191_1","lit_doi_10_48550_arXiv_2510_06182_1","lit_2402_15390_0_1_2_1","lit_2307_15771_0_1_2_1","lit_2605_00269_257_258_259_1","lit_doi_10_48550_arXiv_2307_15771_1"],"found":9,"queries":[{"attacks":"Order-dependent binding IDs make fact-equivalent reordered contexts potentially incompatible activation donors, challenging the fixed ≤12-head intervention’s ≥30% repair/≤3% disruption trade-off.","paper":"How do Language Models Bind Entities in Context? (Jiahai Feng, 2023)","query":"\"How do Language Models Bind Entities in Context?\" binding IDs entity order activation swapping entity attribute misbinding"},{"attacks":"Compensation observed after component ablations shows that downstream computation can counteract local perturbations, an obstacle the sparse donor-head patch must overcome to repair ≥30% of errors.","paper":"The Hydra Effect: Emergent Self-repair in Language Model Computations (Tom McGrath, 2023)","query":"\"The Hydra Effect: Emergent Self-repair in Language Model Computations\" downstream compensation component ablation activation intervention"}],"searched":true,"searches":4},"metadata":{"date":"2026-10-05","problem_id":"515"},"scenario":{"Area":"Lack of adherence: Drift in long contexts, responses to knowledge conflict, incorrect tool use, bias","Description":"AI models when used in a task with long context and workflow can start begin to deviate from its originally specified goals and desired behaviors. These emergent behavior drifts are difficult to predict or detect and may lead to both value and goal misalignment. Interpretability research focuses on the characterization of underlying mechanisms and identification of root causes.","ID":"interp_1","Impact":"Enable early detection of undesired behavior drifts and inform interventions to ensure AI system's persistent adherence to its intended value and goals over long workflow.","Name":{"$ref":"#/scenario/Area"},"Problem IDs":"515,521","Research":"Knowledge conflict detection and characterization,task/goal drift detection and mitigation, monitoring for persona shift and mitigation"}}
